 parallel to the line passing through the points $(2, 1)$ and $(3, 0)$.$y =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487485 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b3f609e-5f7c-5214-b10a-b8eb08f476e6" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487485 #edb/ref 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, both lines should have the same slope.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope of the line passing through the points $(2,1)$ and $(3,0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0 - (1)}{3 - (2)} \\\\\n&= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of\n\n$$\n-1\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(0,3)$. To find the equation of this line, we use the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (3) &= -1(x - (0)) \\\\\ny - (3) &= -x \\\\\ny &= 3 -x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487487 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487487 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487487 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0, 2)$ and is parallel to the line passing through the points $(2, 1)$ and $(3, 3)$.$y =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487487 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "26ebeaaf-47b4-5e9b-a15e-2fa9e9b6c345" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487487 #edb/ref 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, both lines should have the same slope.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope of the line passing through the points $(2,1)$ and $(3,3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 - (1)}{3 - (2)} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of\n\n$$\n2\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(0,2)$. To find the equation of this line, we use the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (2) &= 2(x - (0)) \\\\\ny - (2) &= 2 x \\\\\ny &= 2 x + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487488 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487496 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487488 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487488 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-3, -4)$ and is parallel to the line passing through the points $(2, -1)$ and $(5, -2)$.$y =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487488 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2865c105-cc26-596d-8d65-cb20c88dfa23" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487488 #edb/ref 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, both lines should have the same slope.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope of the line passing through the points $(2,-1)$ and $(5,-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2 - (-1)}{5 - (2)} \\\\\n&= -\\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(-3,-4)$. To find the equation of this line, we use the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-4) &= -\\frac{1}{3}(x - (-3)) \\\\\ny - (-4) &= -\\frac{x}{3} -1 \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{x}{3} -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487490 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487514 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487490 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487490 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0, -1)$ and is parallel to the line passing through the points $(2, 1)$ and $(3, -2)$.$y =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487490 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e11ebc7-3a29-5e32-9ded-4e8408b30cb8" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487490 #edb/ref 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, both lines should have the same slope.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope of the line passing through the points $(2,1)$ and $(3,-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2 - (1)}{3 - (2)} \\\\\n&= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of\n\n$$\n-3\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(0,-1)$. To find the equation of this line, we use the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-1) &= -3(x - (0)) \\\\\ny - (-1) &= -3 x \\\\\ny &= -3 x -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487498 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487504 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487498 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487498 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(3, 4)$ and is parallel to the line passing through the points $(2, -1)$ and $(5, -2)$.$y =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487498 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "845f9834-f22b-53bc-a424-04017a3a9353" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487498 #edb/ref 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, both lines should have the same slope.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope of the line passing through the points $(2,-1)$ and $(5,-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2 - (-1)}{5 - (2)} \\\\\n&= -\\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(3,4)$. To find the equation of this line, we use the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (4) &= -\\frac{1}{3}(x - (3)) \\\\\ny - (4) &= 1 -\\frac{x}{3} \\\\\ny &= 5 -\\frac{x}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487501 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487499 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487501 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186487501 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-3, 3)$ and is parallel to the line passing through the points $(2, -1)$ and $(4, 0)$.$y =$\n{{field-1}}"